  <item>
    <title>Our highest point yet: 15,400 feet (4700 meters)</title>
    <link>https://hobobiker-prep.ddev.site/node/4238</link>
    <description>&lt;div class=&quot;flickrfloat&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;flickrfloat&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;flickr-img-wrapper&quot;&gt;
        &lt;a rel=&quot;lightbox[group1][]&quot; href=&quot;/sites/default/files/2804901882_Nancy_cresting_the_Abra_Yana_Shalla_at_4700_meters_15_400_feet.jpg&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://hobobiker-prep.ddev.site/sites/default/files/imagecache/240x180/2804901882_Nancy_cresting_the_Abra_Yana_Shalla_at_4700_meters_15_400_feet.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; title=&quot;&quot; rel=&quot;lightbox[group1][]&quot; /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;        &lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Today we crossed the highest point of our journey so far, and perhaps the highest of the trip. After climbing back up into the Andes (the Cordillera Blanca) for more than a week, we crossed over 15,400 foot (4700 meter) Abra Yana Shalla. We had been working our way up to this, climbing no more than 1000 meters per day as we got into the really high places. Nancy really felt the altitude in a number of ways, so we were trying to be as careful as possible. But we made the last push this morning up into the barren peaks. We saw lots of beautiful scenery in the last few days and will try to get the pictures uploaded within a few days.&lt;/p&gt;
</description>
